% SOURCE: rddlrepository arcade/TowerOfHanoi/domain.rddl, header verbatim:
% ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
% // The classic Tower of Hanoi puzzle game. Here, disks of various
% // sizes are stacked vertically on a set of rods. Only one disk
% // can be moved at a time, and it can only be placed on top of another
% // disk whose size is greater. The goal is to stack all the disks on
% // a desired rod.
% //
% // Author:
% //    Mike Gimelfarb (mgimelfarb@yahoo.ca)
% //
% ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
%
% Tier-2 investigation (2026-10-09): can the arcade puzzle class be
% metis material? Declarative doctrine applied: the RDDL's int
% fluents (disk-order, disk-count) are ITS ledger — synchronous-DBN
% bookkeeping for "on top" and "stack position". The mechanism is
% relational: a disk RESTS ON a surface (a bigger disk or a rod
% base), a surface is CLEAR or covered — both linear resources; the
% move swaps which surfaces are clear. "Only one disk at a time" =
% one move rule firing; "only on a greater disk" = the fits data.
% 3 disks (d1 < d2 < d3), 3 rods, goal rod r3.
catalog hanoi 1.
provenance text "tower of hanoi: disks stacked on rods, one at a time, never on a smaller disk, all to the target rod" "2026-10-09".
layers (dyn).
stages (play).
type spot {d1 d2 d3 r1 r2 r3}.
namespace th produce (dyn).
pred on(spot, spot) : th.
pred clear(spot) : th.
pred solved : th.
pred tally : th.
bwd fits(spot, spot).
fits(d1, d2).
fits(d1, d3).
fits(d2, d3).
fits(d1, r1).
fits(d1, r2).
fits(d1, r3).
fits(d2, r1).
fits(d2, r2).
fits(d2, r3).
fits(d3, r1).
fits(d3, r2).
fits(d3, r3).
stage play {
  %% "Only one disk can be moved at a time, and it can only be placed on top of another disk whose size is greater." — a clear disk leaves its perch for a clear, fitting surface
  mv [dyn] : $clear(D) * on(D, P) * clear(E) * fits(D, E) -o on(D, E) * clear(P).
  %% "The goal is to stack all the disks on a desired rod."
  done [dyn] : tally * $on(d1, d2) * $on(d2, d3) * $on(d3, r3) -o solved.
}
#init on(d1,d2) 1
#init on(d2,d3) 1
#init on(d3,r1) 1
#init clear(d1) 1
#init clear(r2) 1
#init clear(r3) 1
#init tally 1
#steps 25
